WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.237
Run-to-run variance.

2
00:00:01.517 --> 00:00:03.971
The second source of randomness is the model.

3
00:00:04.251 --> 00:00:11.888
Ask each question once and your score includes the luck of each sample; ask again and the score moves even though nothing changed.

4
00:00:12.168 --> 00:00:23.752
On the current Claude API you can’t remove that by setting temperature to zero, because requests that set sampling parameters are rejected, and other providers’ reasoning models restrict them too.

5
00:00:24.032 --> 00:00:33.248
Even where you can set them, Miller’s advice is not to change sampling settings just to reduce eval variance, since that changes the system you’re measuring.

6
00:00:33.528 --> 00:00:40.376
The fix is to resample: run each question K times and use the per-question mean as that question’s score.

7
00:00:40.656 --> 00:00:58.960
This removes part of the within-question variance; Miller’s worked example shows the variance of the eval mean falling by a third at K = 2 and by half at K = 4, with diminishing returns after that, because the between-question variance doesn’t shrink no matter how often you repeat.

8
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:03.955
Two warnings follow: Don’t pool K times n answers as if they were independent.

9
00:01:04.235 --> 00:01:11.445
Repeated answers to the same question are correlated, and treating them as fresh questions understates the standard error.

10
00:01:11.725 --> 00:01:16.675
Average within each question first, then compute the SE across the n questions.

11
00:01:16.955 --> 00:01:19.429
Decide what “pass” means for your product.

12
00:01:19.709 --> 00:01:28.179
Anthropic’s guide to agent evals distinguishes pass@k (at least one of k trials succeeds) from pass^k (all k succeed).

13
00:01:28.459 --> 00:01:33.109
At a 75 percent per-trial rate, pass^3 is about 42 percent.

14
00:01:33.389 --> 00:01:40.131
A customer-facing agent that must work every time should be judged on consistency, not on its best attempt.

15
00:01:40.411 --> 00:01:51.504
Variance also comes from places you don’t sample on purpose: provider-side changes behind a model alias, retries, tool timeouts, and nondeterministic harness code.

16
00:01:51.784 --> 00:02:05.331
Madaan and colleagues (2024) measured how much benchmark scores vary across models trained from different random seeds and urged practitioners to account for that variance before ranking similar models.

17
00:02:05.611 --> 00:02:12.416
The general lesson transfers: rerun your baseline from time to time to see how much it moves when nothing changes.

18
00:02:12.696 --> 00:02:15.597
That spread is the floor under every difference you’ll claim.
